;; Endeavor 152 rung 11 — the multicast lands the RIGHT BYTES in the workgroup that did NOT
;; fetch them.  On metal.
;;
;; RENAMED from 11-multicast-oneshot-metal, because the one-shot shape it originally used is now
;; known to be UNSOUND for a cluster.  `await` re-initialises the mbarrier inside the loop, so a
;; single re-used barrier lets the leader re-init and issue the next multicast before a peer has
;; re-inited its own — the same race one iteration later.  A RING is the fix, and it is what the
;; real matmul uses anyway.  See 00-verification-findings.md.
;;
;; WHAT THIS RUNG CAN PROVE, and what it cannot.  Rung 10 reads the emitted PTX and shows the
;; multicast INSTRUCTION was issued.  This rung shows the mechanism is CORRECT once issued.
;; Neither replaces the other, and a pass here with rung 10 failing would mean we are measuring a
;; fallback.
;;
;; THE OBSERVABLE IS ATTRIBUTABLE, which is the point of the geometry.  Both workgroups of the
;; cluster load the SAME tile — row-tile 0 of A — because the row coordinate is the constant 0.
;; Each then stores it to its OWN row band of C, keyed by grid-y.  So:
;;
;;     A (4x4, harness fill 0..15)      row0 = 0 1 2 3
;;                                      row1 = 4 5 6 7
;;                                      row2 = 8 9 10 11
;;                                      row3 = 12 13 14 15
;;
;;     tile = A[0:2][0:4]             = 0 1 2 3 / 4 5 6 7
;;     C[0:2] <- tile   (workgroup at grid-y 0)
;;     C[2:4] <- tile   (workgroup at grid-y 1)
;;
;;     => C = 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7
;;
;; ONE OF THOSE TWO WORKGROUPS NEVER ISSUED A FETCH.  Only cluster rank 0 issues the multicast;
;; the other receives it.  So if the multicast fails to reach the non-issuer, exactly one half of
;; C is wrong while the other is right — the failure is attributable to a workgroup rather than
;; showing up as one aggregate that could be wrong for several reasons.
;;
;; TILE SHAPE (2 4) IS FORCED, both numbers by measurement on an H100:
;;   * 4 columns because TMA requires the innermost box dimension to be a multiple of 16 bytes;
;;     a 2-float row is 8 and cuTensorMapEncodeTiled returns `invalid argument`
;;   * 2 rows so the 4-row C yields TWO row-tiles, one per cluster member.  With a taller tile
;;     there is ONE tile, the grid is padded to satisfy cluster divisibility, and the surplus
;;     workgroup exits without initialising its mbarrier — so the multicast targets a dead peer
;;     and the launch fails outright.
;;
;; THE EXPECTATION BELOW IS DERIVED, NOT YET MEASURED.  If it disagrees with hardware, read the
;; arithmetic above before assuming the compiler is wrong — the harness fill and buffer print
;; width are the likely culprits, exactly as they were for rung 04 (which predicted `2 2 2 2` and
;; measured `0 2 4 6`).
;;
;;
;; THE RING SLOT MUST BE INVARIANT ALONG THE CLUSTER AXIS, and getting this wrong DEADLOCKS.
;; The first cut computed `slot` from `grid-y` -- which IS the cluster axis, the one variable
;; that differs between cluster members.  Measured on an H100: the kernel hung with the GPU
;; pegged at 100% and 529 MiB used, i.e. a spin-wait, not a crash.
;;
;; Why: a multicast completes the barrier at ONE offset in every destination workgroup, and
;; writes the data to ONE tile-ring offset in every destination.  With a per-workgroup slot,
;; rank 0 issued against slot 0 while its peer expected and awaited slot 1 -- so the peer waited
;; on a barrier nothing would ever complete, and would have read the wrong tile slot even if it
;; had woken.
;;
;; So the rule is the multicast coordinate rule, one level up: the SLOT expression must not
;; mention the clustered axis's loop variable.  `grid-x` is invariant across this cluster (the
;; members differ only in grid-y), so it is the correct choice.  Rung 22 already used grid-x and
;; is unaffected.
;;
;; HOIST-ARCH: sm_90
;; COMPILE-WITH[--ir-target=ptx --ir-target-arch=sm_90]: PASS
;; TEST-HOIST[CUDA]: validate-cuda-host-run
;; HOIST-EXPECT: BUFFER c: 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7

(def-type mat-t (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel mc_ring_lands (A &out C)
  (declare #'(mat-t &out mat-t)
           (global-size :derive-from C :strategy :strided :tile-shape (2 4))
           (local-size  :set-to 32)
           (cluster-size :set-to (2 1)))
  ;; Rings and barriers bound OUTSIDE tile-stride.  Inside the loop, mbarrier.init and the
  ;; compiler's cluster entry fence would sit on a per-tile trip count, which hangs the moment
  ;; two workgroups of a cluster claim different numbers of tiles.  The shipped chapter-3 kernel
  ;; binds its barriers outside for the same reason.
  (let ((tile-ring (make-scratch-matrix-ring float (2 4) :ring-count 2))
        ;; data arrival stays WORKGROUP-LOCAL: a multicast completes its transaction on each
        ;; destination's OWN mbarrier, so :block describes it exactly and :arrivals is unscaled.
        (full  (make-async-barrier-ring :ring-count 2 :mode :block   :arrivals 1))
        ;; buffer-free is the ring that needs cluster reach: peers must tell the issuer a slot is
        ;; free.  :arrivals 1 is per-workgroup; the compiler scales it by the group extent.
        (empty (make-async-barrier-ring :ring-count 2 :mode :cluster :arrivals 1)))
    (tile-stride C (2 4) (grid-y grid-x)
      (let ((slot (mod (to-ulong grid-x) (to-ulong 2))))
        ;; row coordinate is the CONSTANT 0 -> both workgroups want the same tile -> multicast
        (load-tile A (ring-get tile-ring slot) (0 grid-x)
                   :barrier (ring-get full slot) :multicast true)
        (await (ring-get full slot))
        (store-tile (ring-get tile-ring slot) C (grid-y grid-x))
        (signal (ring-get empty slot))))))
